# spi_display_rx: the receiving end of a display's four-wire SPI link —
# `sclk`, `mosi`, `dc` and `cs_n` — oversampled in the system clock
# domain.
#
# There is no datasheet for this link. Everything the block assumes is
# what the user observed with an analyser on their own hardware: `sclk`
# in bursts of exactly eight edges, one data wire carrying commands and
# pixels, a `dc` wire that holds for a whole byte and says which, and a
# fourth wire high between bursts. README.md §1 records all three passes
# of that observation, including the two readings that were wrong, and
# §5 says which parameters a measurement still has to choose, §4 how to
# read every counter and §6 the order to suspect things in when they come
# back wrong.
#
# Receive only: no `miso` and no transmit path.
#
# `sclk` is **not** a clock here. All four pins are synchronised through
# `cdc_sync` and oversampled, so the whole block sits in one clock domain
# and its bytes cross nothing. That costs a rate limit, stated as a ratio
# because nothing here knows what `clk` runs at: an `sclk` period must
# last at least `2 * PHASE_MARGIN` system clocks, four at the default, so
# `sclk` may be up to a quarter of `clk`. `overrun_count` reports when
# the margin is gone rather than dropping bits quietly. The block's
# header has the whole argument.
#
# **Nothing in this block has been near a part.** It is verified in
# simulation and by arithmetic, and README.md says so where a reader
# cannot miss it.
#
# The counters are the point as much as the bytes are:
# `cmd_byte_count` against `data_byte_count` says what a capture
# contained, and `bit_error_count` and `dc_change_count` are numbers that
# should read **zero** against this link.
name spi_display_rx
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "Receiver for a display's four-wire SPI link: one data wire sampled against an oversampled external clock, bytes framed by the chip select and tagged by D/C, with counters for command and data bytes, frames, bit-count mismatches, D/C disagreements and sampling overruns"

top spi_display_rx
target ice40
target ecp5

source rtl/spi_display_rx.v

depends cdc_sync ^1.0.0

param SAMPLE_EDGE int 0 0..1
param MSB_FIRST int 1 0..1
param FRAME_MODE int 0 0..2
param CS_ACTIVE_LOW int 1 0..1
param CS_PULSE int 0 0..1
param DC_SAMPLE int 0 0..2
param DC_DATA_LEVEL int 1 0..1
param PHASE_MARGIN int 2 1..8
param SYNC_STAGES int 2 2..4
param COUNT_WIDTH int 16 1..32

port clk in
port rst_n in
port sclk in
port mosi in
port dc in
port cs_n in
port rx_byte out 8
port rx_is_data out
port rx_valid out
port cmd_byte_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port data_byte_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port frame_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port bit_error_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port dc_change_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port overrun_count out COUNT_WIDTH
port bit_count out 4
port last_bit_count out 4
port framed out
port framing_error out
port dc_error out
port overrun out
